  "query_preview": "..(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 330 AM EST WED NOV 4 2026\n\nAnother disturbance will trek from the Mid-Mississippi Valley and \nmove towards the OH/TN valleys to begin the long term period. At the \nsame time, a longwave trough over the Great Lakes region will \nmeander southward towards the Ohio River. This trough will cross the \narea to end the week and bring a shot of colder, drier air as we \nhead into the weekend. Following highs in the 60s to near 70 on \nFriday, temperatures will drop into the 50s for highs Saturday and \ninto the 30s for lows particularly in valleys on Sunday morning. \nMonday morning appears to be the coldest, with lows well into the \n30s for most locations. Lingering cloud cover may impact how cold \nthe sheltered valleys get, but it is not out of the question that \nsome of the more sheltered valley locations may see lows in the 20s \nMonday morning. Due to the cold morning temperatures, frost appears \nlikely.\n\nAt this point, a fairly quiet synoptic pattern is currently \nanticipated from early to the middle portion of next week, with \nsurface high pressure building across the Ohio Valley. Model \nguidance has some spread in terms of the positioning and strength of \ntroughing across the southeast CONUS early next week. The 00Z ECMWF \nand the 0Z ECMWF ensemble mean have ridging in the southeast to \nbegin the week. The 0Z GFS and 0Z GFS ensemble mean keep a trough in \nthe southeast at that time. Both scenarios would result in very \nminimal or no chances for rain from early to the middle of next \nweek. The consensus is for temperatures to hovering near normal, \nwith daytime highs in the 60s and nighttime lows in the 30s.",
